cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Noophorics: a quantitative science of transferring understanding across heterogeneous minds"
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
type: software
version: 0.5.0
date-released: 2026-08-03
# Must move with `version:` above. It said 2026-07-30 at v0.5.0 -- the
# version bumped three times and the release date stayed put, which is the
# same class of defect as a stated count that disagrees with its source,
# in the one file a citation manager reads.
# Founded 2026-07-28 by Valentyn Yakymenko.
license: Apache-2.0
url: "https://noophorics.org"
repository-code: "https://github.com/vyakymenko/noophorics"
abstract: >-
  Noophorics is a research program that measures the transfer of understanding
  between systems with non-identical priors — human to model, model to model,
  and a session to its own compacted successor. It replaces symbol recovery
  with behavioral convergence as the criterion of successful transfer, which
  yields measurable quantities: transfer fidelity relative to a stated probe
  measure *and a stated reference disposition*, noophoric efficiency, channel capacity between minds, and phantom
  agreement — the gap between the parties' confidence that understanding
  occurred and the evidence that it did. The repository contains the axiomatic
  foundation, six falsifiable conjectural laws, fifteen open problems, a
  dependency-free reference implementation of the metrics, and pre-registered
  experiments. As of v0.5 one pre-registered result stands: the calibration
  slope `beta` -- how far a party's stated confidence moves when the outcome
  moves -- measured at 0.13 pooled against 1.0 for calibration, with the
  sender's slope indistinguishable from zero.
keywords:
  - noophorics
  - transfer fidelity
  - phantom agreement
  - probe measure
  - agent handoff
  - context compaction
  - multi-agent systems
  - indeterminacy of translation
  - calibration slope
  - pre-registration
authors:
  - family-names: "Yakymenko"
    given-names: "Valentyn"
    website: "https://github.com/vyakymenko"
